[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 2 > **UE Logic for AI** (EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **UE Logic for AI** (EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: M. Tettamanzi
- The objective of this course is to provide a general background in Logic to enable access to more advanced topics in computer science and, more specifically, in artificial intelligence. Intelligent systems in the real world have to deal with issues like uncertainty, imprecision, and incompleteness. Some prominent logical tools that can be used to approach these issues are thus discussed.
- **Lectures**
- Part I. Basics
- Propositional Logic: syntax and semantics
- First Order Predicate Logic: syntax and semantics
- Natural Deduction
- Unification and Resolution
- Part II. Non-Monotonic Logic and Approximate Reasoning
- Argumentation Theory
- Belief Revision and Update
- Fuzzy Logic
- Possibility Theory

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 1 > **Web** (EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **Web** (EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: M. Buffa
- It has also become a “social machine” and a technological
infrastructure for collective intelligence, which constitutes an
interesting and complex subject of study.
The objective of this course is to provide a comprehensive introduction
to the architecture, standards, languages, and models that allow this
huge distributed system to function, without forgetting its societal
impact.
- Lectures
- The Web’s Architecture and protocols
- A refresher on HTML, CSS, and the Document Object Model
- The Common Gateway Interface and Server-side Programming
- Client-side Programming (JavaScript and the HTML5 API)
- Persistence, AJAX, and REST
- An introduction to Web Services, UDDI, and SOAP
- Ergonomy
- Web Science: The Web as an object of study.

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 2 > **Modelling Distributed Systems**(EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **Modelling Distributed Systems**(EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Mme. Di Giusto
- The aim of this course is to introduce mathematical formalisms for modelling and reasoning about properties of distributed, reactive systems.
Topics may include models of message passing and shared memory concurrency (Petri nets, process algebras, communicating and timed automata) as well as formalisms to reason on such systems (bisimulation techniques, session types, logics, invariants…).

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 1 > **UE AI Game Programming (EN)** - 3 ECTS]
# Cours Programme 2026/2027  
## Semestre 1  
### **UE AI Game Programming (EN)** - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: M. Regin
- This course presents different techniques to program games for one or two players. For two-player games, only full information games will be considered.
- The students will have to develop, in groups of two, a program that will simulate a human player.
A competition between the programs will be organized and it will determine the notes.

---

[Context: Cours Programme 2026/2027 > Semestre 2 > **UE Introduction to Multiagent Systems** (EN) - 3 ECTS]
### **UE Introduction to Multiagent Systems** (EN) - 3 ECTS  
- Lecteur: M. Leturc
- This course provides an introduction to the fundamental concepts, models, and applications of Multi-Agent Systems (MAS).
Students will learn how autonomous agents interact, cooperate, and compete within distributed environments. Topics include the taxonomy and architectures of MAS, coordination and communication mechanisms, decision-making under uncertainty, and social dynamics such as voting, reputation, and argumentation. The course also introduces elements of planning, game theory, and computational social choice theory, bridging theoretical models of MAS with practical applications.

---

